(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-evidence-quiz"]={"title":"Evidence Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which challenge arises when two sound studies disagree?","choices":["Publication bias","Measurement bias","Conflicting evidence","Outdated evidence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside evidence being simply unavailable or outdated, so the clinician may face too little evidence, too old, or too contradictory. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How does the United States Preventive Services Task Force define certainty?","choices":["The likelihood that its assessment of net benefit is correct","The probability that the preventive service will help a given patient","The proportion of studies that agree with one another","The statistical significance of the pooled effect estimate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and net benefit is defined as benefit minus harm of the preventive service as implemented in a general primary care population. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which of these is named as an evidence-based medicine database?","choices":["TripPro","The National Health Interview Survey","The Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance Survey","The Florida Cancer Data System"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside Clinical Key and Essential Evidence Plus, which incorporates InfoPOEMS and InfoRetriever. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What did Archie Cochrane advocate?","choices":["The core concepts and definition of evidence-based medicine","Randomised controlled trials and systematic reviews","The use of expert opinion to settle clinical disputes","Grading recommendations from A through D and I"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and he advocated them specifically as tools for verifying whether a treatment actually works, which is the question the whole field is built around. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why are higher-level studies more likely to show that an outcome is due to what was studied?","choices":["They enrol patients from a single centre","They are least vulnerable to bias","They are published in higher-impact journals","They report larger effect sizes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systematic error is what allows something other than the exposure or intervention to produce the observed outcome, so reducing it strengthens the attribution. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why does pooling data across studies usually help?","choices":["It removes any bias present in the individual studies","It converts observational studies into experiments","A greater number of participants usually means greater power","It guarantees the result will be statistically significant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the qualifier usually is deliberate: pooling poor studies produces a larger poor study rather than a reliable answer. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why does understanding study design matter for evidence-based practice?","choices":["Knowing each design's strengths and limits improves evaluation of evidence","It allows the clinician to replicate the study","It determines which journal will publish the work","It removes the need to read the results section"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the chain runs further than that: strong design produces strong data, which produces valid and reliable results, which supports better clinical decisions. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does a meta-analysis do?","choices":["Pools data from multiple studies to produce a single large study","Identifies and evaluates studies to answer a focused question","Follows a single cohort forward to observe outcomes","Grades the strength of a clinical recommendation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the reason it is worth doing is power: a greater number of participants usually means a greater ability to detect a real effect. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What should a patient offered a Grade I service understand?","choices":["That the service has been shown to be harmful","That the benefit is small but real","That the evidence is strong and consistent","That the balance of benefits and harms is uncertain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the clinician is directed to the clinical considerations section of the recommendation statement before deciding whether to offer it at all. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which two grades carry the same instruction to offer or provide the service?","choices":["B and C","A and B","C and D","D and I"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because both rest on a net benefit judged at least moderate. Grade C offers selectively, Grade D discourages, and Grade I calls for the clinical considerations to be read. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does the scale of the published literature imply for the clinician?","choices":["Every clinical question now has a definitive published answer","Searching the literature has become unnecessary","Only the largest journals need be consulted","Volume alone does not settle clinical questions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: more than two million studies, thirty thousand journals, a quarter of a million controlled trials of therapy and over nineteen million citations, and the answers are still incomplete. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What does the breadth of the list of information sources imply?","choices":["All listed sources carry equal evidential weight","Only electronic databases should be consulted","Information reaches clinicians from many channels of unequal quality","Clinical experience is excluded as a source"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: it runs from original research journals and evidence-based databases through experts and guidelines to newsletters, representatives, media and patients. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Where and when did evidence-based medicine originate?","choices":["Oxford University in the 1950s","The United States Preventive Services Task Force in the 1980s","McMaster University in the 1970s","The Cochrane Collaboration in the 1990s"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two figures are named as key contributors from there onward: David Sackett, whose work gave the field its core concepts and definition, and Archie Cochrane. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How is net benefit defined?","choices":["The proportion of patients who improve after the service","The difference in cost between offering and withholding the service","The statistical significance of the effect in the pooled analysis","Benefit minus harm in a general primary care population"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the population matters: the service is assessed as implemented in general primary care rather than in the selected setting of a trial. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"In the ABCD method for appraising a website, what does A stand for?","choices":["Accuracy of the statistics presented","Affiliation of the hosting institution","Author, including credentials and expertise","Availability of the full text"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the other three follow: bias, content in the sense of level of detail, and date, meaning whether the material is still current. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What question does descriptive evidence answer?","choices":["Which test most accurately predicts the diagnosis","Which treatment is most effective for the diagnosis","Whether the diagnosis can be prevented","What characterises patients with a specific diagnosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It describes who has the condition rather than evaluating how it is detected or what should be done about it, which are the other two kinds of evidence named. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does the B of the ABCD method ask about?","choices":["Breadth of coverage","Bibliography","Bias","Backing institution"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it sits between the author's credentials and the level of detail in the content, so the method moves from who wrote it to why, then to what and when. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the general lesson of the retracted vaccine study?","choices":["Systematic reviews are more reliable than single studies","Publication in a respected journal does not guarantee integrity","Vaccine research should not be published","Retraction always follows a failure to replicate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It appeared in a major journal and was eventually retracted, which is why appraisal of the author, the interests behind the work and the methods still matters. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Where do critically appraised individual articles sit relative to randomised trials?","choices":["Below them","At the same level","Above them","Below cohort studies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the appraisal has already been done. They are also called article synopses, and sit below critically appraised topics and systematic reviews. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"In the ABCD method, what does D address?","choices":["The depth of detail the source provides","Whether the author has a conflict of interest","The database in which the source is indexed","Whether the material is still current"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it matters because outdated evidence is one of the named challenges of evidence-based practice: a source can be sound and still be superseded. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Beyond expertise and external evidence, what else does evidence-based practice consider?","choices":["The reputation of the journal publishing the evidence","The individual patient's situation and preferences","The cost to the health system of each alternative","The preference of the treating institution"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why evidence alone is not enough: the same evidence can support different decisions for two patients who value the outcomes differently. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What distinguishes a meta-analysis from a systematic review?","choices":["It combines the data rather than only appraising the studies","It asks a broader question","It includes only randomised controlled trials","It is primary rather than secondary research"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the review identifies and evaluates the studies, and the meta-analysis pools their data to produce what functions as one larger study. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which are named as challenges to implementing evidence-based medicine?","choices":["Evidence-based medicine ignores the individual patient","Searching takes time, and evidence may be absent or conflicting","There are too few published studies to draw on","Clinicians are not permitted to access medical databases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and a fifth is about communication rather than the evidence itself: findings are often presented in language clinicians may have difficulty understanding. Outdated evidence is a fourth. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which appraisal test for websites is provided by the library?","choices":["The ABCD method alone","The five As","The clinical studies pyramid","The CRAAP test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the ABCD method sits alongside it, asking after the author, bias, content and date of any web-based material. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which question is posed as the reason for evidence-based practice?","choices":["How do you know that what you do really works?","How can care be delivered at the lowest cost?","How can clinicians keep up with the literature?","Which treatments do most clinicians currently use?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is deliberately uncomfortable: it asks not whether a practice is common or familiar, but whether there is reason to believe it achieves anything. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which factors constrain confidence at MODERATE certainty?","choices":["Study number, size or quality, inconsistency, and limited generalisability","A complete absence of any relevant studies","Consistent results across well-conducted representative studies","Disagreement among the task force members"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, together with a lack of coherence in the chain of evidence. As more information becomes available, the magnitude or direction of the effect could change enough to alter the conclusion. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What does Evidence Based Medicine Reviews contain?","choices":["Only systematic reviews produced by the Cochrane Collaboration","Seven leading evidence-based resources, plus Medline","Practice guidelines issued by federal agencies","Point-of-care summaries written for bedside use"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it carries over two hundred full-text journals alongside those resources, which is what makes it a single entry point rather than one more database. Over two hundred full-text journals come with them. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What did the retracted vaccine and autism study demonstrate about undeclared interests?","choices":["The study was retracted purely because others could not replicate it","Peer review reliably prevents this kind of failure","The conflict was declared but judged unimportant","An undisclosed payment and a competing patent lay behind the work"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the author had been paid to advise lawyers representing parents, and had filed a patent for a competing vaccine without disclosing either. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which agency provides access to the United States Preventive Services Task Force?","choices":["The Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality","The National Library of Medicine","The Cochrane Collaboration","The National Center for Health Statistics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and two other guideline sources are named with it: the Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report recommendations, and the Task Force on Community Preventive Services. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What does the validity step of evidence-based practice assess?","choices":["Whether the research applies to this patient","Whether the research is true","Whether the outcome improved after the change","Whether the question was correctly framed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it comes before application rather than after: research whose truth has not been established should not be carried into a patient encounter. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"}]};
